---
import BaseLayout from "../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ProblemsSidebar from "../../../../components/ProblemsSidebar.astro";
import Math from "../../../../components/Math.astro";
import Equation from "../../../../components/Equation.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Lagrange Identities"
  description="A survey of the classical linear algebra formula."
>
  <section class="la-page">
    <ProblemsSidebar />

    <article class="topic-article">
      <header class="topic-hero">
        <span class="eyebrow">Algebra · Identities</span>
        <h1>Lagrange Identities</h1>

        <p>
	  A survey of the classical linear algebra formula.
        </p>
      </header>

      <nav class="topic-toc" aria-label="Page sections">
        <a href="#broad-stroke">Broad Stroke</a>
        <a href="#quick-examples">Quick Examples</a>
        <a href="#a-deeper-look">A Deeper Look</a>
      </nav>

      <section class="topic-section" id="round-i">
        <h2>Round I</h2>

	<p>
	  Let there be <Math tex="x_i, y_i \in \mathbb{R}, i = \overline{1, 3}"/>. One textbook problem I once stumbled upon in high school asked of me to prove the following
	  two identities (which are in fact particular cases of the general Lagrange formula we'll discuss in a bit):

	  <Equation
	    tex={String.raw`(x_1^2 + x_2^2)(y_1^2 + y_2^2) = (x_1y_1 + x_2y_2)^2 + (x_1y_2 - x_2y_1)^2,`}
	  />
	  
	  <Equation
	    tex={String.raw`(x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2)(y_1^2 + y_2^2 + y_3^2) - (x_1y_1 + x_2y_2 + x_3y_3)^2 =
	    (x_1y_2 - x_2y_1)^2 + (x_2y_3 - x_3y_2)^2 + (x_3y_1 - x_1y_3)^2.`}
	  />

	  Try maybe crafting some neat proofs for each (maybe a unique proof for the first one and then use it to prove the second one). If you're thinking of raw expansion here,
	  that'd probably be a waste of time and energy; you can just skip to the general case and see the direct algebraic proof.

	  
	</p>
	
	<details class = "drop-example">
	  <summary>Proof</summary>
	  
	  <div class = "drop-content">
	    <p> Okay, these two particular proofs presented here don't have to be what you would've come up with necessarily, they're just for the fun of it since base cases usually
	    leave more room for creativity and exploration. The first proof might be interesting. The second one maybe not so much, it just makes some shortcuts. </p>


	    <details class="drop-example">
	      <summary>First One</summary>

	      <div class="drop-content">
		
		<p> We're going to use <span class="emph"> complex algebra </span>
		  (you might notice Lagrange's identity seems to have something to do with the concept of
		  <span class="emph"> magnitude </span> of, say, a complex number, or a vector - more on that later btw).
                Consider two complex numbers, <Math tex="z = x_1 + x_2i"/> and <Math tex="w =y_1 - y_2i"/>.
		Remember the complex moduli of each is expressed as <Math tex="|z| = \sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2}"/> and <Math tex="|w| = \sqrt{y_1^2 + y_2^2}"/> respectivelly.
                Take the product between <Math tex="z"/> and the conjugate of <Math tex="w"/>: </p>

		<Equation
		  tex={String.raw`z\overline{w} = (x_1y_1 + x_2y_2) - (x_1y_2 - x_2y_1)i.`}
		/>

		<p> Now all we have to do is take the modulus of both the <span class="emph"> LHS </span> and <span class="emph"> RHS </span>, and make use of the fact that
                <Math tex="|z\overline{w}| = |z||\overline{w}|"/>,
                which is going to yield the result we are looking for. </p>
		
	      </div>

	    </details>

	    <details class="drop-example">
	      <summary>Second One</summary>

	      <p> Again, what we want to prove is this: </p>

	      <Equation
		tex={String.raw`({\red x_1^2 + x_2^2} + x_3^2)({\red y_1^2 + y_2^2} + y_3^2) - ({\red x_1y_1 + x_2y_2} + x_3y_3)^2 =
		(x_1y_2 - x_2y_1)^2 + (x_2y_3 - x_3y_2)^2 + (x_3y_1 - x_1y_3)^2.`}
	      />

	      <p> The highlighted parts in the <span class="emph"> LHS </span> are already present in the first formula we've proven. Let's use that: </p>

	      <Equation
		tex={String.raw`\text{LHS } = \underbrace{(x_1^2 + x_2^2)(y_1^2 + y_2^2) - (x_1y_1 + x_2y_2)^2}_{(x_1y_2 - x_2y_1)^2}
		+ (x_1^2 + x_2^2)y_3^2 + (y_1^2 + y_2^2)x_3^2 - 2(x_1y_1 + x_2y_2)x_3y_3,`}
	      />

	      <p> and then notice how we can express the second half of the expression: </p>

	      <Equation
		tex={String.raw`(x_1^2 + x_2^2)y_3^2 + (y_1^2 + y_2^2)x_3^2 - 2(x_1y_1 + x_2y_2)x_3y_3 =
		\underbrace{(x_2y_3)^2 - 2(x_3y_2)(x_2y_3) + (x_3y_2)^2}_{(x_2y_3 - x_3y_2)^2} + \underbrace{(x_3y_1)^2 - 2(x_3y_1)(x_1y_3) + (x_1y_3)^2}_{(x_3y_1 - x_1y_3)^2}.`}
	      />

	      <p> Putting it all together gives the desired result. </p>

	      <div class="drop-content">

	      </div>

	    </details>
	    
	  </div>
	  
	</details>
	
      </section>

      <section class="topic-section" id="round-ii">
        <h2>Round II</h2>

	<p>
	  Tackling the proofs in the previous section is probably a healthy exercise. We proved the base case and then moved on to a higher order case to maybe form some sort of
	  idea as to how to handle increasing the number of variables indefinitely. So proving the general formula, which by now you can probably infer that it should look something
	  like this (for <Math tex="x_i, y_i \in \mathbb{R}, i = \overline{1, n}"/>):

	  <Equation
	    tex={String.raw`\left(
            \sum_{i = 1}^{n}x_i^2 \right)\left( \sum_{i = 1}^{n}y_i^2 \right) - \left( \sum_{i = 1}^{n}x_iy_i \right)^2 = \sum_{1 \leq i,j \leq n}(x_iy_j - x_jy_i)^2,`}
	  />

	  is going to require a strategy that's pretty similar to what we did before.
	  
	</p>
	
	<details class="drop-example">
	  <summary>Proof</summary>

	  <p> The proof itself shouldn't be to tough to wrap one's mind around, but it requires some level of comfort with the <Math tex="\Sigma"/> notation. We'll simply start from
	  the <span class="emph"> LHS </span> and make our way to the <span class="emph"> RHS </span> with relative ease. First, we are going to expand the two products: </p>

	  <Equation
	    tex={String.raw`\text{LHS} = \left(
            \sum_{i = 1}^{n}x_i^2 \right)\left( \sum_{i = 1}^{n}y_i^2 \right) - \left( \sum_{i = 1}^{n}x_iy_i \right)\left( \sum_{i = 1}^{n}x_iy_i \right)
	    = \sum_{1 \leq i,j \leq n}(x_iy_j)^2 - \sum_{1 \leq i,j \leq n}x_iy_jx_jy_i.`}
	  />

	  <p> Basically, if you think about what we do when we expand the product of two parentheses, we are forming every possible pair between a term in the first parenthesis and
	    a term from the second parenthesis. So indices <Math tex="i"/> and <Math tex="j"/> range from <Math tex="1"/> to <Math tex="n"/>, generating all pairs, which is what
	    the <Math tex="\sum_{1 \leq i,j \leq n}"/>  notation means, thus turning two sums into one, and the summand itself is simply the product between the two original summands
            (so <Math tex="(x_iy_i)^2"/> comes from multiplying together <Math tex="x_i^2"/> and <Math tex="y_i^2"/> and <Math tex="x_iy_jx_jy_i"/> comes from multiplying together
            <Math tex="\text{ }x_iy_i"/> and <Math tex="x_jy_j"/> - then we simply rearanged terms conveniently).
	  </p>

	  <p> Since the two resulting sums have identical ranges, we can combine them into one: </p>

	  <Equation
	    tex={String.raw`
            \text{LHS}
	    = \sum_{1 \leq i,j \leq n}((x_iy_j)^2 - x_iy_jx_jy_i).`}
	  />

	  <p>
	    Notice how the summand becomes <Math tex="0"/> when <Math tex="i = j"/>, so we could technically specify that <Math tex="i \neq j"/> beneath the sum notation to avoid
	    redundancy, but ultimately it's inconsequential. Now we're almost there, but the <span class="emph"> RHS </span> we want to arrive at is a sum of squares,
            <Math tex="(x_iy_j - x_jy_i)^2"/>, while our current summand, <Math tex="(x_iy_j)^2 - x_iy_jx_jy_i"/> is half a square. Since <Math tex="i"/> and <Math tex="j\text{ }"/>
	    form together all possible pairs of indices ranging from <Math tex="1"/> to <Math tex="n"/>, every <Math tex="(i, j)"/> pair has a corresponding <Math tex="(j, i)\text{ }"/>
	    pair, meaning every <Math tex="(x_iy_j)^2 - x_iy_jx_jy_i"/> term has a corresponding <Math tex="(x_jy_i)^2 - x_jy_ix_iy_j"/> term somewhere in the sum. Combining them,
	    we get:
	  </p>

	  <Equation
	    tex={String.raw`(x_iy_j)^2 - x_iy_jx_jy_i + (x_jy_i)^2 - x_jy_ix_iy_j = (x_iy_j)^2 - 2x_iy_jx_jy_i + (x_jy_i)^2 = (x_iy_j - x_jy_i)^2.`}
	  />

	  This is exactly the summand we were looking for and the proof is done.

	  <div class="drop-content">
	    
	  </div>
	  
	</details>
	
      </section>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
